{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ColoringStrategy"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"]}],"kind":"declarations"}],"relationshipsSections":[{"type":"conformsTo","kind":"relationships","title":"Conforms To","identifiers":["doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s12CaseIterableP","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/SQ","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/SH","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s16SendableMetatypeP"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule"]]},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"How "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"greedyColoring(strategy:)"},{"type":"text","text":" orders the vertices. Each vertex in turn takes the least colour"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"none of its already coloured neighbours has (first fit). Degrees are simple degrees (distinct"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"other neighbours, self-loops left out); ties go to the lesser vertex index, except for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".coloredNeighbors"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"topicSections":[{"anchor":"Enumeration-Cases","identifiers":["doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/coloredNeighbors","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/connectedSequentialBreadthFirst","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/connectedSequentialDepthFirst","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/independentSet","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/largestFirst","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/saturationLargestFirst","doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/smallestLast"],"title":"Enumeration Cases","generated":true},{"anchor":"Default-Implementations","identifiers":["doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/Equatable-Implementations"],"title":"Default Implementations","generated":true}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"kind":"symbol","schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"sections":[],"metadata":{"symbolKind":"enum","externalID":"s:14ColoringModule0A8StrategyO","roleHeading":"Enumeration","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ColoringStrategy"}],"remoteSource":{"fileName":"Coloring.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/ColoringModule\/Coloring.swift#L109"},"title":"ColoringStrategy","modules":[{"name":"ColoringModule"}],"fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ColoringStrategy","kind":"identifier"}]},"references":{"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/Equatable-Implementations":{"kind":"article","abstract":[],"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/Equatable-Implementations","title":"Equatable Implementations","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/equatable-implementations","role":"collectionGroup"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/smallestLast":{"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/smallestlast","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/smallestLast","title":"ColoringStrategy.smallestLast","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"identifier","text":"smallestLast"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Matula–Beck: repeatedly remove a vertex of least degree in what is left (the least index on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ties), then colour in the reverse of that removal order. At most degeneracy + 1 colours, so"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"at most 6 on a planar graph. O((n + m) log n)."}],"kind":"symbol"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/connectedSequentialDepthFirst":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/connectedSequentialDepthFirst","type":"topic","role":"symbol","title":"ColoringStrategy.connectedSequentialDepthFirst","abstract":[{"text":"The same in depth-first preorder. O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"connectedSequentialDepthFirst","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/connectedsequentialdepthfirst","kind":"symbol"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/saturationLargestFirst":{"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"saturationLargestFirst","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"DSatur (Brélaz 1979): first a vertex of greatest degree, then repeatedly the uncoloured"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex with the most distinct colours among its neighbours, ties to the greatest degree in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the whole graph, then to the lesser index. Exact on bipartite graphs. O((n + m) log n)."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/saturationLargestFirst","title":"ColoringStrategy.saturationLargestFirst","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/saturationlargestfirst","type":"topic","role":"symbol"},"doc://ColoringModule/s16SendableMetatypeP":{"type":"unresolvable","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s16SendableMetatypeP","title":"Swift.SendableMetatype"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/largestFirst":{"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"largestFirst"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Degree descending, the lesser index first on ties: Welsh–Powell, which colours class by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"class in this order and gives the same colouring as first fit. O(n + m)."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/largestFirst","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/largestfirst","type":"topic","title":"ColoringStrategy.largestFirst","role":"symbol"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule":{"type":"topic","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule","title":"ColoringModule","kind":"symbol","role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule"},"doc://ColoringModule/SH":{"type":"unresolvable","title":"Swift.Hashable","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/SH"},"doc://ColoringModule/SQ":{"type":"unresolvable","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/SQ","title":"Swift.Equatable"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy":{"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ColoringStrategy"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ColoringStrategy"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"How "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"greedyColoring(strategy:)"},{"type":"text","text":" orders the vertices. Each vertex in turn takes the least colour"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"none of its already coloured neighbours has (first fit). Degrees are simple degrees (distinct"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"other neighbours, self-loops left out); ties go to the lesser vertex index, except for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".coloredNeighbors"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"title":"ColoringStrategy","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy","role":"symbol"},"doc://ColoringModule/s8SendableP":{"type":"unresolvable","title":"Swift.Sendable","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s8SendableP"},"doc://ColoringModule/s12CaseIterableP":{"type":"unresolvable","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s12CaseIterableP","title":"Swift.CaseIterable"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/coloredNeighbors":{"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/coloredneighbors","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"First the vertex of greatest degree (the least index on ties), then repeatedly the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"uncoloured vertex with the most coloured neighbours. Ties among those fall as a two-way"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"indexed binary max-heap breaks them, not by index: vertices are pushed in index order and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"raised neighbour by neighbour in ascending order; an entry moves above an equal parent and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"sinks to the left child unless the right one is strictly greater. O((n + m) log n)."}],"title":"ColoringStrategy.coloredNeighbors","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"coloredNeighbors"}],"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/coloredNeighbors"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/independentSet":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/independentSet","type":"topic","role":"symbol","title":"ColoringStrategy.independentSet","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Colour classes one at a time: class k is a maximal independent set of the vertices not yet"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"coloured, built by repeatedly taking the one with the fewest neighbours among those still"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"available (the least index on ties) and making it and its neighbours unavailable."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"O(χ (n + m) log n) for χ classes."}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"independentSet"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/independentset","kind":"symbol"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/connectedSequentialBreadthFirst":{"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/connectedsequentialbreadthfirst","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Components by least vertex, each in breadth-first order from its least vertex, neighbours in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"incidentEdges","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" order. O(n + m)."}],"title":"ColoringStrategy.connectedSequentialBreadthFirst","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"connectedSequentialBreadthFirst","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/connectedSequentialBreadthFirst"}}}